WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360
"Which Ultra-Processed Foods Drive
the Association with Death and Disease the Most?"

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.640
Even a small increase

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.640
in the consumption
of ultra-processed foods

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.960
is associated
with an increased risk

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.280
of developing several diseases.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.280
And for each additional
daily serving,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.520
the risk of dying prematurely
may increase by 2%.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.280
Are there any babies we’d be
throwing out with the bathwater

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.520
if we avoided
ultra-processed foods?

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.880
Are there any that haven’t
been associated with disease?

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.560
It’s such a big category.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.720
What we need are studies that
break it down and here we go.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.040
The Framingham Offspring study:

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.880
Higher consumption
of ultra-processed foods

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.480
is associated
with increased risk

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.320
of cardiovascular disease.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.000
But was it because
of the burgers or the bagels?

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.680
The link between
ultra-processed foods

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.560
and disease was largely driven
by the ultra-processed meat,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.880
the sausage, bologna, salami,
hot dogs, and burgers.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.080
Breakfast cereals, nearly all
of which are ultra-processed,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.480
were associated with lower risk,
presumably

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.520
because cereal eaters
were eating less bacon and eggs.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.120
In another US study,
this one 30 times larger,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.760
researchers came
to the same conclusion.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.800
The more ultra-processed,
the more cardiovascular disease.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.200
But which ultra-processed foods?

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.200
Soft drinks,
both regular and diet,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.560
and meat, specifically burgers,
fried chicken,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.720
fried fish, and meat pizza.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.600
Salty snacks like corn chips
and potato chips, and candy.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.080
Ultra-processed food consumption
and high blood pressure

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.880
driven mostly by soda and meat.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.880
If we excluded soda and meat,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.400
would there still
be an association

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.360
between ultra-processed foods
and cardiovascular disease?

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.080
We can turn to the three big
Harvard cohorts.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.880
200,000 people followed
for decades.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.600
And yes, total intake
of ultra-processed foods

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.080
is associated with
cardiovascular risk,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.440
but that was driven
by soda and meat.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.120
Sugar-sweetened beverages
and processed red meat,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.080
poultry and fish,
like fish sticks.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.720
What if we exclude soda
and meat?

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.880
The relationship between
ultra-processed foods

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.720
and cardiovascular disease
disappears.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.200
So, nutritional advice
for cardiovascular health

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.600
should consider
the differential consequences

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.240
of group-specific
ultra-processed foods

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.920
and specifically, that means
soft drinks and processed meats

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.160
should be discouraged.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.400
You know what else
is an ultra-processed food?

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.680
Vodka.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.520
So, when you look
at food processing

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.880
and cancer risk,
if you exclude alcoholic drinks,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.800
all of a sudden,
the link between processed foods

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.520
and a number of cancers
becomes insignificant,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.640
suggesting that it was probably

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.360
the alcohol driving
those associations.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.240
Though for colorectal
cancer risk, soda,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.080
and ready-to-eat foods
like pizza,

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.480
and ready-to-eat meat, poultry,
and fish,

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.400
may also be playing a role.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.600
For pancreatic cancer,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.200
the association between
ultra-processed foods

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.200
and pancreatic cancer
was really more

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.640
just an association with foods
like donuts, cakes, cookies,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.800
and meat and meat products.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.920
Diabetes.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.240
You can see the straight line
increase in diabetes risk

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.320
with increasing
ultra-processed food intake,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.800
but not all
ultra-processed foods:

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.960
mostly animal-based products
and the pizza category again.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.960
What about mortality?

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.200
Those with the most
ultra-processed food consumption

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.400
had a higher risk of dying
from all causes put together,

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.360
but it mattered
what kinds of foods.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.560
Ready-to-eat red meat, poultry,
and seafood products

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.320
had particularly strong
associations with mortality.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.240
The worst ultra-processed food

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.200
when it came to dying
prematurely in general,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.040
meat, poultry, seafood.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.040
The worst when it comes to dying
from cancer,

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.400
meat, poultry, seafood.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.200
The worst when it comes to dying
from cardiovascular disease,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.320
meat, poultry, seafood.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.640
The worst when it comes to dying
from lung disease,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.040
like Emphysema,
meat, poultry seafood.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.480
The worst when it comes to dying
from neurodegenerative diseases,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.440
ice cream.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.600
And the worst when it comes
to dying from other causes,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.520
meat, poultry, seafood.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.920
The bottom line
is that the major factors

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.680
contributing
to the harmful influence

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.760
of ultra-processed foods
on mortality

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.000
are soda and processed meat,
including poultry and fish.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.520
That’s what a systematic review

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.480
and meta-analysis
of all such studies showed:

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.760
higher mortality associated

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.760
with drinking
more sugar sweetened beverages

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.840
like soda, including diet soda,
and eating more processed meat,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.720
or eating any processed meat
at all.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.160
So, the ultra-processed
foods problem may really

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.760
just be a soda and meat problem.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.120
There was one ray of sunshine
in this study, though.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.080
The researchers found one
ultra-processed dessert

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.880
that was associated
with lower mortality-

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.664
dark chocolate.

